CH = dict(num=6, title="The Rise of Empires", tag="History", theme="Empires, Magadha, Alexander, Mauryas, Kautilya, Ashoka",
 intro="Between the 6th and 2nd centuries BCE, small kingdoms merged into large empires, led by Magadha. This chapter explains what an empire is, how Magadha, the Nandas and the Mauryas rose, how Alexander's invasion touched the north-west, what Kautilya taught about governance, why Ashoka chose peace, and why empires are fragile.",
 big_questions=["What is an empire?", "How did empires rise and shape Indian civilisation?", "What factors helped kingdoms become empires?", "What was life like from the 6th to the 2nd century BCE?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "6.1 What Is an Empire?"),
 ("ul", ["'Empire' comes from Latin **imperium** = supreme power. An empire is a collection of smaller kingdoms or territories under a powerful ruler (or group), often won by war. The smaller territories keep their own rulers but are **tributaries** (vassals) to the **emperor**, who rules from a capital that is an economic and administrative centre.",
  "A **tributary** pays **tribute** (money, gold, grain, livestock, elephants, goods) as a sign of submission and accepts the emperor's **overlordship**.",
  "Sanskrit terms for emperor: **samraj** (lord of all), **adhiraja** (overlord), **rajadhiraja** (king of kings)."]),
 ("h3", "Features of an empire"),
 ("ol", ["Emperor exerts **central authority** over tributary kings.",
  "Keeps an **army** to control tributaries, expand and guard against attack.",
  "Maintains an **administration** (officials who govern territories, collect taxes, keep law and order).",
  "Builds **communication networks** (roads, river and sea routes) and infrastructure for trade and welfare.",
  "Makes **laws**, issues **currency**, weights and measures, regulates trade.",
  "**Controls resources**: mines, forests, farm produce, manpower.",
  "Encourages arts, literature, religions, schools of thought and centres of learning."]),
 ("ul", ["**Why become an emperor?** ambition to rule the 'whole world' and be remembered by posterity; control resources to build economic and military strength; wealth.",
  "**How empires grow**: trained armies conquer neighbours; fortified settlements on borders; war on neighbours; control of rivers and trade networks (resources and tax); the kingdom with stronger military and surplus resources becomes overlord."]),
 ("h2", "6.2 Trade, Trade Routes and Guilds"),
 ("ul", ["An army is costly (food, pay, weapons, horses, elephants, roads), so empires need strong **production and trade**. More trade → more income for producers and more tax for the king.",
  "Traded goods: textiles, spices, farm produce, gems, handicrafts, animals; many Indian goods went overseas by land and sea. Major routes: **Uttarapatha, Dakshinapatha**; cities such as Pataliputra, Ujjayini, Taxila, Mathura, Kaushambi, Tamralipti, Bharukachchha, Kanchipuram, Madurai, Muchiri.",
  "**Guilds (shrenis)** were associations of traders, craftsmen, moneylenders or farmers, headed by an elected head and executive officers. They (1) turned competitors into **collaborators** sharing market information and (2) had **autonomy** to frame their own rules; the king did not interfere. They lasted centuries and show India's **self-organising ability** (as do village councils)."]),
 ("h2", "6.3 The Rise of Magadha"),
 ("ul", ["In the 6th-4th centuries BCE, **Magadha** (south Bihar and nearby) rose above the 16 mahajanapadas; **Ajatashatru** was an early powerful king. The Buddha and Mahavira lived in his time.",
  "**Why Magadha?** resource-rich **Ganga plains** with fertile soil, timber forests and elephants; **iron ore** from nearby hills (iron ploughs raised output; lighter, sharper weapons strengthened the army); the **Ganga and Son** rivers aided transport and trade; surplus food let artisans flourish.",
  "**Mahapadma Nanda** (5th century BCE) founded the **Nanda dynasty**, united many kingdoms, issued coins and kept a large army (per Greek accounts). The last Nanda, **Dhana Nanda**, was rich but oppressive and unpopular, so the empire fell to the Mauryas.",
  "Sanskrit grammarian **Panini** (5th century BCE) composed the **Ashtadhyayi**, 3,996 concise **sutras** on grammar."]),
 ("h2", "6.4 The Arrival of the Greeks"),
 ("ul", ["North-west India had smaller kingdoms such as that of the **Pauravas** under **Porus**.",
  "**Alexander** of Macedonia (334-331 BCE) conquered the Persian Empire, spreading Greek influence over three continents. **327-325 BCE**: he entered India, defeated Porus in Punjab (the Battle of the **Hydaspes**) but faced fierce resistance and lost men; his soldiers, tired and homesick, refused to go further towards the Ganga. He retreated, losing many in the harsh coastal/desert route. He died in **Babylon** at 32 (**324-323 BCE**) and his empire was split among his generals and **satraps** (governors of provinces).",
  "Porus, asked how he wished to be treated, replied '**Like a king**'; Alexander restored him as satrap.",
  "**Gymnosophists** ('naked philosophers'), Indian sages, answered Alexander's riddles calmly and impressed him — a meeting of Greek and Indian thought.",
  "Impact: limited political effect but opened **Indo-Greek cultural contacts**."]),
 ("h2", "6.5 The Mighty Mauryas"),
 ("ul", ["About **321 BCE**, **Chandragupta Maurya**, helped by mentor **Kautilya** (Chanakya / Vishnugupta, teacher at **Taxila**), overthrew Dhana Nanda (who insulted Kautilya, who vowed to end the Nandas) and founded the **Maurya Empire** with **Pataliputra** (modern Patna) as capital.",
  "He defeated the Greek satraps in the north-west; the empire stretched from the northern plains to the Deccan. Greek ambassador **Megasthenes** lived in his court and wrote **Indika** (lost, known from quotations)."]),
 ("h3", "Kautilya's Arthashastra"),
 ("ul", ["*Arthashastra* = 'science of governance and economics': defence, economy, administration, justice, town planning, agriculture, welfare, anti-corruption laws.",
  "**Saptanga** (seven parts of a kingdom) is shown below.",
  "He stressed law and order, welfare of the **countryside** (source of economic activity), and said: 'In the happiness of his subjects lies the king's happiness' — the people's interests come first."]),
 ("table", ["Part", "Meaning"], [
  ["Swami", "The king"], ["Amatya", "Ministers, councillors and officials"], ["Janapada", "Territory and its population"],
  ["Durga", "Fortified towns and cities"], ["Kosha", "Treasury"], ["Danda", "Forces of defence and law and order"], ["Mitra", "Allies"]]),
 ("h2", "6.6 Ashoka: The King Who Chose Peace"),
 ("ul", ["**Ashoka (268-232 BCE)**, Chandragupta's grandson, expanded the empire over almost all the Subcontinent except the far south, including today's Bangladesh, Pakistan and parts of Afghanistan.",
  "The **Kalinga war** (modern Odisha) brought huge death and destruction; Ashoka gave up violence and adopted the Buddha's **peace and non-violence**. He admitted this in his edicts.",
  "He sent **emissaries** to Sri Lanka, Thailand, Central Asia and beyond to spread the Buddha's message.",
  "**Edicts** (official declarations) were engraved on rocks and pillars in **Prakrit** using the **Brahmi** script (mother of Indian scripts) at Girnar, Sarnath, Sanchi, Dhauli, Lumbini, Kandahar and others; Ashoka is called a 'great communicator'. He styled himself **Devanampiya Piyadasi** (Beloved of the Gods; one who regards all with kindness).",
  "Welfare claims: medical care for people and animals, ban on hunting and cruelty to animals (early conservation), rest houses, wells and shade trees on roads, encouraging all sects to respect each other. An edict tells officers that all men are his children, to be impartial and never imprison or torture without cause; an officer toured every five years.",
  "**Dharma (dhamma)**: moral law and duty; at a deeper level, living in harmony with the cosmic order (*ritam*): duty, law, truth, order and ethics.",
  "After Ashoka the empire survived about 50 years, then weak successors let regions break away; around **185 BCE** a new phase began."]),
 ("h2", "6.7 Life in the Mauryan Period"),
 ("ul", ["Cities such as Pataliputra had palaces, public buildings and planned streets; a strong **taxation system** and trade kept the treasury rich.",
  "**Sohgaura copper plate** (4th-3rd century BCE, Prakrit, Brahmi): one of the earliest administrative records, about a **granary** for famine relief (food security).",
  "From Megasthenes: most people farmed; **two crops a year**; famines rare; granaries well stocked; farmers were left undisturbed even in wartime. Artisans (blacksmiths, potters, carpenters, jewellers) lived in planned cities with street signs; couriers carried messages; wooden houses up to two storeys; water vessels kept for fire; cotton clothes and thick-soled leather shoes."]),
 ("h3", "Contributions of the Mauryas"),
 ("table", ["Area", "Details"], [
  ["Art and architecture", "Highly polished stone pillars; **Sarnath lion capital** with four lions (royal power), an elephant, bull, horse and lion on the ring and the **dharmachakra**; **Great Stupa, Sanchi** (originally brick, later stone); stupas, chaityas, viharas; rock-cut caves at Barabar Hills; Dhauli elephant sculpture"],
  ["Symbols today", "Sarnath capital = **national emblem** with motto **Satyameva Jayate** ('truth alone triumphs', *Mundaka Upanishad*); dharmachakra on the national flag"],
  ["Economy", "Strong trade routes, extensive punch-marked coins, tax system"],
  ["Urban life and administration", "Well-designed towns, elaborate administration"],
  ["Terracotta art", "Dancing girl, female deity, horse head, Saptamatrikas"]]),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**Stupa**: dome-shaped structure holding relics. Its hemispherical dome is the **anda** (the universe); people walk around it clockwise (**pradakshina**) in worship."),
 ("h2", "6.8 The Fragile Nature of Empires"),
 ("ul", ["Reasons for decline: regions seeking independence when tribute demands rise (long wars, drought); weak successors; the larger an empire, the harder to hold (distant areas split first); economic crises from floods and droughts.",
  "Paradox: empires bring **political unity**, peace among small states and prosperity, but are built by **war and force**, making them unstable."]),
 ("table", ["Period (BCE)", "Event"], [
  ["600-500", "Mahajanapadas; rise of Magadha (Ajatashatru)"],
  ["5th century", "Mahapadma Nanda founds the Nanda dynasty; Panini"],
  ["327-325", "Alexander in north-west India"],
  ["321", "Chandragupta Maurya founds the Maurya Empire"],
  ["268-232", "Ashoka's reign; Kalinga war"],
  ["185", "End of the Mauryas"]]),
 ("h2", "6.9 Quick Revision"),
 ("qa", [("What is an empire? How does it differ from a kingdom?", "An empire is a large territory of many kingdoms ruled by an emperor, with tributary kings; a kingdom is a single state under its own king."),
  ("What is tribute?", "Money, gold, grain, animals or goods paid by a vassal state to the emperor as a sign of loyalty."),
  ("Why did Magadha become powerful?", "Fertile Ganga plains, forests, elephants, iron ore, rivers for trade and a strong army."),
  ("What are guilds? Why important?", "Associations of traders and craftsmen with elected heads and autonomy; they encouraged cooperation and kept trade strong."),
  ("Who were the Nandas and why did they fall?", "A dynasty founded by Mahapadma Nanda; the last king, Dhana Nanda, was oppressive and unpopular, and was overthrown by Chandragupta."),
  ("What was the effect of Alexander's invasion?", "Limited political impact; opened Indo-Greek contacts; his soldiers refused to proceed beyond Punjab."),
  ("Who was Kautilya? What is the saptanga?", "Mentor of Chandragupta and author of the Arthashastra; saptanga lists seven parts of a state: king, ministers, territory, forts, treasury, army and allies."),
  ("Who was Megasthenes?", "Greek ambassador at Chandragupta's court who wrote Indika."),
  ("Why did Ashoka give up war?", "The death and destruction of the Kalinga war changed him; he adopted the Buddha's path of non-violence and dharma."),
  ("How did Ashoka spread his message?", "Through edicts in Prakrit/Brahmi on rocks and pillars and emissaries sent abroad."),
  ("What did Ashoka do for welfare?", "Medical care, rest houses and wells, shade trees, protection of animals, tolerance of all sects."),
  ("Which Mauryan symbols are used today?", "The Sarnath lion capital as the national emblem, with Satyameva Jayate; dharmachakra on the flag."),
  ("Why are empires fragile?", "They rest on force; distant regions rebel; weak rulers, tribute burdens or disasters cause break-up.")]),
 ],
 takeaways=["Empire = many kingdoms under one emperor; vassals pay **tribute**.",
  "**Magadha**: Ganga plains, iron, rivers → Nandas → **Mauryas (321 BCE)**.",
  "**Guilds** and trade routes funded empires.",
  "**Alexander (327-325 BCE)** reached Punjab; no lasting rule in India.",
  "**Kautilya's Arthashastra** and **saptanga** shaped Mauryan governance.",
  "**Ashoka** (268-232 BCE): Kalinga war → peace, dharma, edicts in Prakrit/Brahmi.",
  "Legacy: **national emblem**, dharmachakra, Sanchi, roads and coins; empires are strong yet **fragile**."])
